|~ qlang operand catalog — control family. Each operand BindStep
   below sits directly under the per-site error tags it raises;
   the operand's `:throws` Vec references those tags by name. ~|

:if
  |~~ Three-branch conditional: `if cond ~(then) ~(else)`. The
      condition is a value computed at the call, each branch a quote
      applied to pipeValue, and only the selected branch runs.

      ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "positive")
      ~(-1 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "non-positive")
      ~({:age 25} | if (/age | gte 18) ~("adult") ~("minor") | eq "adult")
      ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 100) ~() | eq 105)

      The condition answers a boolean; a value of another kind is
      refused, since a condition that is not a boolean has no second
      reading. The empty quote `~()` is the branch that leaves
      pipeValue as it is. ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/if
            :category :control
            :subject :any
            :returns :any
            :modifiers [:any :pipeline :pipeline]
   }

::IfConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The condition of `if` answers a boolean; null, a number, a string
      or any other value is refused where it stands.
      ~(
        5 | if 1 ~("yes") ~("no")
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IfConditionNotBooleanError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::IfThenNotQuoteError
  |~~ The then branch of `if` is code it runs only when chosen, so it
      takes a quote, `if (gt 0) ~("yes") ~("no")`; every branch is checked at the call.
      ~(
        5 | if true "yes" ~("no")
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IfThenNotQuoteError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::IfElseNotQuoteError
  |~~ The else branch of `if` is code it runs only when chosen, so it
      takes a quote, `if (gt 0) ~("yes") ~("no")`; every branch is checked at the call.
      ~(
        5 | if true ~("yes") "no"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IfElseNotQuoteError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

:coalesce
  |~~ Walks the captured alternatives in order, evaluating each
      against pipeValue, and returns the first result that is
      neither null nor an error value. Every other value —
      false, 0, "", [], {}, #[] among them — short-circuits the
      iteration. All-skip chain yields null. Use the
      `coalesce ~(/key) ~(fallback)` shape to read "use /key when
      defined, else fallback" — `/key` produces an error on a
      missing-key Map, which the skip rule treats the same as
      null.

      ~({:nickname null :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) | eq "alice")
      ~({:nickname "ace" :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) | eq "ace")
      ~({:a 2 :b 3} | coalesce ~(/missing) ~("fallback") | eq "fallback")
      ~({:nickname null :name null} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) ~("anonymous") | eq "anonymous")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/coalesce
            :category :control
            :subject :any
            :returns :any
            :modifiers [:pipeline]}

::CoalesceNoAlternativesError
  |~~ `coalesce` requires at least one captured alternative sub-pipeline.
      ~(
        {} | coalesce
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::CoalesceNoAlternativesError]
       )
  ~~|

::CoalesceAlternativeNotQuoteError
  |~~ Every alternative of `coalesce` is code it runs only when reached, so
      each takes a quote, `coalesce ~(/b) ~(/a)`; `:index` counts the modifiers from 1.
      ~(
        {:a 1} | coalesce /a
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::CoalesceAlternativeNotQuoteError 1 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:cond
  |~~ Multi-way dispatch over alternating (predicate, branch)
      sub-pipeline pairs. Walks captured args in pairs; returns
      the result of the first branch whose predicate answers true
      against pipeValue, each predicate answering a boolean. If
      captured-arg count is odd, the trailing arg is the default
      branch. If even and no match, returns null. Replaces
      nested-if chains with a flat catalog.

      ~(85 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "B")
      ~(65 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "F")
      ~({:role "admin"} | cond ~(/role | eq "admin") ~("full") ~(/role | eq "user") ~("limited") | eq "full")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/cond
            :category :control
            :subject :any
            :returns :any
            :modifiers [:pipeline]}

::CondNoBranchesError
  |~~ `cond` requires at least one (predicate, branch) pair plus an optional trailing default.
      ~(
        5 | cond
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::CondNoBranchesError]
       )
  ~~|

::CondConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ Every predicate of `cond` answers a boolean; `:index` counts the
      modifiers from 1 and names the predicate that answered another
      kind.
      ~(
        5 | cond ~(1) ~("yes")
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::CondConditionNotBooleanError 1 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::CondClauseNotQuoteError
  |~~ Every clause of `cond` is code it runs only when reached, so
      each takes a quote, `cond ~(gt 0) ~("positive") ~("other")`; `:index` counts the modifiers from 1.
      ~(
        5 | cond true ~("yes")
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::CondClauseNotQuoteError 1 ::boolean]
       )
  ~~|
